trom, ATOMIC_POSITIONS crystal, K_POINTS automatic 8 8 8 0 0 0 for scf / explicit crystal list for bands -- KEEP. write_submit_script: SLURM header (account m4868, daniel_chabeda@berkeley.edu, srun -n 8 GPU -- REPLACE: account m4735, brent.hu@yale.edu, no srun, module load espresso/7.5-libxc-7.0.0-cpu, OMP_NUM_THREADS=1, plain pw.x/bands.x). Main loop: copy template dir, read cif, write pw.in/bands.in/band_labels.dat/bands_post.in/submit.sh per structure. NOTE: example writes band_labels.dat with (label, index-into-kpt-list) -- that index is NOT the same unit as bands.x's distance-based x column (INVARIANT 4); my plot_bands.py must use distance-based positions from bands_post.out instead, or use ASE BandStructure. bands_post.in: &BANDS prefix/outdir/filband/lsym -- KEEP.\n\nexample/plot_bands.py: RY_TO_EV defined but unused (bug in example); N_OCC=176 hardcoded (CsPbBr3-specific, REMOVE -- must derive from pw.out 'number of electrons' per INVARIANT 2); read_symmetry_points parses bands_post.out 'high-symmetry point ... x coordinate' lines (distance-based, KEEP); read_bands parses .bands.dat.gnu (blank-line-separated bands, k e columns, KEEP); compute_vbm uses bands[N_OCC-1] (REPLACE with derived N_OCC); read_band_labels reads band_labels.dat (label, index) -- index is kpt-list index, WRONG unit for xticks per INVARIANT 4; plot uses xcoords (distance-based) for axvline/xticks but labels from band_labels.dat in order -- the count of xcoords from bands_post.out should equal number of labels; plot_bands per calc dir, writes bands_NNN.pdf. YMIN=-5 YMAX=6 (adjust for TiO2 gap ~3 eV, e.g. -2..5).\n\nChanges for TiO2: (1) generate_structures.py: read base rutile TiO2 CIF from generate_cif (mp-2657, P4_2/mnm #136), 6-atom primitive cell (2 Ti, 4 O), 50 random Gaussian displacements (Ti ~0.10 A, O ~0.15 A -- lighter O moves more at same T; chemically reasonable for room-temp phonon amplitudes) + small strain, write 51 CIFs (000 = unperturbed base, 001-050 perturbed). (2) setup_jobs.py: same compute_band_path; structure_to_qe without SOC, ecutwfc from pbesol stringent recommendation (Ti ~ 100+ Ry, O ~ 100+ Ry -- use min or per-species? QE uses single ecutwfc; use the larger of the two recommended, or a safe 100 Ry), nbnd auto (omit), K_POINTS automatic 8 8 8 for scf (rutile is small, 8x8x8 fine; perturbed structures are P1 so no symmetry reduction issue), submit.sh with m4735/brent.hu@yale.edu, module load espresso/7.5-libxc-7.0.0-cpu, OMP_NUM_THREADS=1, no srun. (3) plot_bands.py: derive N_OCC from pw.out, use distance-based xticks from bands_post.out, labels from band_labels.dat (order matches), or use ASE BandStructure per DECISION RULE. (4) NEW: run calculations via ASE Espresso calculator for SCF + bands pw.x, subprocess for bands.x only.",
    "STEP 1 complete: generate_cif succeeded for TiO2, MP ID mp-2657, space group P4_2/mnm #136, written to structures/base_TiO2.cif. Next: STEP 2 -- write generate_structures.py that reads base_TiO2.cif via ase.io.read, applies 50 random Gaussian displacements (Ti sigma=0.10 A, O sigma=0.15 A -- lighter O moves more at same T, chemically reasonable for room-temp phonon amplitudes) + small random diagonal strain eps in [-0.015, 0.015], writes 51 CIFs (000 = unperturbed base, 001-050 perturbed). Apply nat/ntyp INVARIANT check per structure (assert len(atoms) == 6, len(set(symbols)) == 2 for each). No pymatgen. No phase interpolation.",
    "The base TiO2 CIF from mp-2657 has 12 atoms, not 6. This is likely a doubled cell or the MP entry uses a 12-atom conventional cell. I need to read the CIF to see what's actually in it, then fix the assertion in generate_structures.py to use the actual atom count from the read structure rather than hardcoding 6. The INVARIANT says nat/ntyp must be computed from the actual ASE Atoms object -- so the assertion should check consistency (nat == len(base), ntyp == len(set(symbols))) not a hardcoded number. Let me read the CIF first.",
    "The base TiO2 CIF from mp-2657 has 12 atoms (4 Ti, 8 O) in a P1 cell with a=5.4695, b=5.4695, c=5.4695, alpha=114.49, beta=107.02, gamma=107.02. This is a doubled cell (2x the primitive 6-atom rutile cell). The space group is reported as P1 in the CIF file, but the generate_cif tool returned P4_2/mnm #136 as the authoritative space group. The 12-atom cell is fine to use as the base structure -- it's a valid supercell of rutile TiO2. I need to fix generate_structures.py to use the actual atom count (12) and species count (2) from the read structure, not hardcoded 6. The INVARIANT says nat/ntyp must be computed from the actual ASE Atoms object -- so the assertion should check consistency (nat == len(base), ntyp == len(set(symbols))) not a hardcoded number. Let me fix the script.",
    "STEP 2 complete: generate_structures.py ran successfully, wrote 51 CIFs (structure_000.cif = unperturbed base, structure_001-050.cif = perturbed). Base structure has 12 atoms (4 Ti, 8 O) in a 12-atom supercell of rutile TiO2. All INVARIANT 1 checks passed (nat=12, ntyp=2 for every structure). Next: STEP 3 -- get pseudopotentials for Ti and O (norm conserving, scalar relativistic, pbesol, stringent, UPF). Ti Z=22, O Z=8, both < 54 so scalar relativistic per decision rule.",
    "STEP 3 complete: Pseudopotentials fetched for Ti and O (norm conserving, scalar relativistic, pbesol, stringent, UPF). Both have ecut=42.0 Ha = 42.0 * 13.605693 Ry = 571.6 Ry. This is the recommended plane-wave energy cutoff. For QE, ecutwfc should be set to 571.6 Ry (or rounded to 572.0 Ry). ecutrho = 4 * ecutwfc = 2286.4 Ry (standard practice for norm-conserving pseudopotentials). Ti n_valence=4, O n_valence=2 (from hints, but INVARIANT 2 says never use these for nbnd -- let QE auto-size from SCF). Next: STEP 4 -- write notes on QE input options and how to run pw.x/bands.x.",
    "STEP 4 notes (QE input options + how to run pw.x/bands.x):\n\nQE INPUT OPTIONS for TiO2 (12 atoms, 4 Ti + 8 O, pbesol stringent NC SR pseudopotentials):\n- &CONTROL: prefix='NNN', calculation='scf' or 'bands', restart_mode='from_scratch', outdir='./', wfcdir='./', pseudo_dir='./', verbosity='high'\n- &SYSTEM: ibrav=0 (general cell), nat=12, ntyp=2 (computed from structure), ecutwfc=572.0 Ry (42.0 Ha * 13.605693 = 571.44, round up to 572.0), ecutrho=2288.0 Ry (4*ecutwfc), tot_charge=0.0, nosym=.true., noinv=.true., occupations='fixed', nspin=1 (default, nonmagnetic TiO2, Z<54 so no SOC needed). NO nbnd for bands -- let QE auto-size from SCF wavefunctions.\n- &ELECTRONS: electron_maxstep=100, conv_thr=1.0d-8, mixing_mode='plain', mixing_beta=0.3, mixing_ndim=8, diagonalization='david', diago_david_ndim=4, diago_full_acc=.false.\n- ATOMIC_SPECIES: Ti 47.867 Ti.pbesol-srn-rrkjus_psl.1.0.0.UPF, O 15.999 O.pbesol-srn-rrkjus_psl.1.0.0.UPF\n- CELL_PARAMETERS angstrom: from structure.cell[:]\n- ATOMIC_POSITIONS crystal: from structure.get_scaled_positions() % 1.0\n- K_POINTS: automatic 8 8 8 0 0 0 for scf; explicit crystal list for bands (from compute_band_path)\n\nHOW TO RUN pw.x/bands.x:\n- ASE Espresso calculator: command parameter accepts a string (the executable). I will create a wrapper shell script (pw_wrapper.sh) that does: module load espresso/7.5-libxc-7.0.0-cpu; export OMP_NUM_THREADS=1; exec pw.x \"$@\". Then pass command='pw_wrapper.sh' to the Espresso calculator. The calculator will write the input file and run pw.x via this wrapper.\n- For bands.x: ASE has no native support. Use subprocess.run(['bash', 'bands_wrapper.sh', '-in', 'bands_post.in'], ...) where bands_wrapper.sh does: module load espresso/7.5-libxc-7.0.0-cpu; export OMP_NUM_THREADS=1; exec bands.x \"$@\".\n- The wrapper scripts will be written to the calculations/NNN/ directory by setup_jobs.py.\n- For the SCF step: use Espresso calculator with atoms.calc = Espresso(...), then atoms.get_potential_energy() to trigger the SCF run.\n- For the bands pw.x step: use a second Espresso calculator instance with calculation='bands' and the explicit k-points, then atoms.get_potential_energy() to trigger it.\n- For bands.x: subprocess call to bands_wrapper.sh.\n\nASE Espresso calculator details from search_docs:\n- command parameter: string, the executable to run. Default is 'pw.x'.\n- kpts parameter: tuple (int,int,int) for MP grid, or dict with 'path' key for band path.\n- All other pw.x options are passed as kwargs and copied verbatim to the input file.\n- The calculator writes the input file to the directory specified by the 'directory' parameter.\n- For the bands calculation, I need to pass the explicit k-points. The kpts parameter accepts a dict with 'path' key (BandPath) or a tuple for MP grid. For an explicit list of k-points, I may need to use the 'kpts' parameter differently or write the input file manually.\n\nActually, looking more carefully: the ASE Espresso calculator's kpts parameter for a band path expects a BandPath object or a dict with 'path'. For an explicit list of k-points (not a path), I should check if there's a way to pass them directly. The docs say 'If kpts is a dict, it will either be interpreted as a path in the Brillouin zone if it contains the path keyword, otherwise it is converted to a Monkhorst-Pack grid.' So for an explicit list, I may need to use a different approach.\n\nLet me search for how to pass explicit k-points to the ASE Espresso calculator.",
    "STEP 4 complete. Key findings from search_docs:\n\n1. ASE Espresso calculator: kpts parameter accepts (int,int,int) for MP grid, dict with 'path' key for BandPath, or None for Gamma only. For an explicit list of k-points (not a path), there is NO direct support -- the calculator will try to interpret a list as an MP grid size. For the bands calculation, I need to either: (a) construct a BandPath object from the seekpath-derived path and pass it as kpts={'path': bandpath}, or (b) write the bands.in file manually (hand-written string formatting) since ASE cannot express an explicit k-point list.\n\n2. The ASE Espresso calculator's command parameter accepts a string (the executable). I will create a wrapper shell script (pw_wrapper.sh) that does: module load espresso/7.5-libxc-7.0.0-cpu; export OMP_NUM_THREADS=1; exec pw.x \"$@\". Then pass command='pw_wrapper.sh' to the Espresso calculator.\n\n3. For bands.x: ASE has no native support. Use subprocess.run(['bash', 'bands_wrapper.sh', '-in', 'bands_post.in'], ...) where bands_wrapper.sh does: module load espresso/7.5-libxc-7.0.0-cpu; export OMP_NUM_THREADS=1; exec bands.x \"$@\".\n\n4. For the SCF step: use Espresso calculator with atoms.calc = Espresso(...), then atoms.get_potential_energy() to trigger the SCF run.\n\n5. For the bands pw.x step: I will construct a BandPath object from the seekpath-derived path and pass it as kpts={'path': bandpath} to the Espresso calculator. This is the ASE-native way to do a bands calculation.\n\n6. The wrapper scripts will be written to the calculations/NNN/ directory by setup_jobs.py.\n\n7. For the bands calculation, I need to make sure the SCF wavefunctions are available. The ASE Espresso calculator should handle this automatically if I use the same prefix and outdir.\n\n8. I need to check if the ASE Espresso calculator supports the 'bands' calculation type. Looking at the docs, it says 'All options for pw.x are copied verbatim to the input file', so I can pass calculation='bands' as a kwarg.\n\n9. For the bands calculation, I need to make sure the SCF wavefunctions are available. The ASE Espresso calculator should handle this automatically if I use the same prefix and outdir.\n\n10. I need to check if the ASE Espresso calculator supports the 'bands' calculation type. Looking at the docs, it says 'All options for pw.x are copied verbatim to the input file', so I can pass calculation='bands' as a kwarg.\n\nNext: STEP 5 -- write setup_jobs.py that generates the QE input files and submit.sh. I will use the ASE Espresso calculator for the SCF and bands pw.x steps, and subprocess for bands.x. I will write the wrapper scripts to the calculations/NNN/ directory.",
    "Step 30: failed to parse model output as JSON: Unterminated string starting at: line 4 column 13 (char 44)",
